title: One wire per number: a decoder
mode: vhdl
chapter: Buses and numbers
check: behaves
---- intro
A **decoder** turns a number into one wire per value: the 2-bit input `a` (0 to 3) lights exactly one of the four outputs `y(0)` to `y(3)`. It is how a computer picks one memory chip, or one register, from a number.

`with ... select` gives a value for each case, like a table:

```
with a select y <=
    "0001" when "00",
    "0010" when "01",
    ...
```
---- task
Light `y(0)` for `a = "00"`, `y(1)` for `"01"`, `y(2)` for `"10"` and `y(3)` for `"11"`.
---- start
library ieee;
use ieee.std_logic_1164.all;

entity decoder is
  port(
    a : in  std_logic_vector(1 downto 0);
    y : out std_logic_vector(3 downto 0)
  );
end decoder;

architecture rtl of decoder is
begin
    -- write it here
end rtl;
---- answer
library ieee;
use ieee.std_logic_1164.all;

entity decoder is
  port(
    a : in  std_logic_vector(1 downto 0);
    y : out std_logic_vector(3 downto 0)
  );
end decoder;

architecture rtl of decoder is
begin
    with a select y <=
        "0001" when "00",
        "0010" when "01",
        "0100" when "10",
        "1000" when others;
end rtl;
---- hint1
Four cases, one line each; the last can be `when others`, which covers anything left.
---- hint2
The values are `"0001"`, `"0010"`, `"0100"` and `"1000"`: the 1 moves left as `a` grows.
---- solution
```
with a select y <=
    "0001" when "00",
    "0010" when "01",
    "0100" when "10",
    "1000" when others;
```
---- done
`with ... select` is the text version of a truth table. Next: choosing with a table.
